WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.915
The study guide asks for endotracheal tube size by height and weight.

2
00:00:05.915 --> 00:00:13.293
The first thing to know is that adults are not sized that way at all: adults are sized by sex.

3
00:00:13.293 --> 00:00:19.257
Children are sized by an age formula, or by height with a length-based tape.

4
00:00:19.257 --> 00:00:25.614
Neonates are sized by weight, or by gestational age when the weight is not known.

5
00:00:25.614 --> 00:00:32.913
For each group we cover the size, the insertion depth, and the checks that confirm placement.

6
00:00:32.913 --> 00:00:41.075
One gap remains: no source gives an adult size by height or weight, or the Broselow tape's color values.

7
00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:49.889
Tube size is the internal diameter in millimeters, and tubes come in sizes two to ten.

8
00:00:49.889 --> 00:01:00.714
For this exam, use RCP 203 Lesson 6a: women seven point zero to eight point zero, and men eight point zero to nine point zero.

9
00:01:00.714 --> 00:01:09.906
RCP 105 and Hess give a narrower seven to seven point five for women and eight to eight point five for men.

10
00:01:09.906 --> 00:01:24.168
The oral depth at the teeth is nineteen to twenty one centimeters for women and twenty one to twenty three for men, but those are initial estimates, not confirmation.

11
00:01:24.168 --> 00:01:37.828
Hess secures the tube at twenty one for women and twenty three for men until the chest film, where the tip should sit four to six centimeters above the carina.

12
00:01:37.828 --> 00:01:42.725
Have at least one size larger and one size smaller ready.

13
00:01:44.133 --> 00:01:49.509
Use a tube of size eight or larger when the patient's size allows it.

14
00:01:49.509 --> 00:02:04.312
An endotracheal tube raises the work of breathing by fifty four percent to two hundred forty percent, depending on the tube size and the flow, so a narrow tube costs the patient real effort.

15
00:02:04.312 --> 00:02:09.220
A tube that is too small is also a cause of high cuff pressure.

16
00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:10.544
And watch a trap.

17
00:02:10.544 --> 00:02:18.958
The deck sizes the laryngeal mask airway by weight and the King LT by height, but not the endotracheal tube.

18
00:02:20.367 --> 00:02:23.768
For children, RCP 203 gives two formulas.

19
00:02:23.768 --> 00:02:28.331
An uncuffed tube is the age divided by four, plus four.

20
00:02:28.331 --> 00:02:35.383
A cuffed tube is the age divided by four, plus three point five, half a size smaller.

21
00:02:35.383 --> 00:02:42.684
The deck's example is a four year old: five point zero uncuffed, four point five cuffed.

22
00:02:42.684 --> 00:02:55.958
Hess chapter twenty two gives the same uncuffed formula for children one to ten years old, and an insertion depth from the lips of the age plus ten centimeters.

23
00:02:55.958 --> 00:03:14.542
Applying those rules, the study guide works out a two year old at four point five uncuffed, four point zero cuffed, twelve centimeters deep, and an eight year old at six point zero, five point five, and eighteen centimeters.

24
00:03:15.967 --> 00:03:28.551
For sizing by height, the Broselow tape is a color-coded, length-based tape that recommends emergency equipment, including airways.

25
00:03:28.551 --> 00:03:36.428
Length-based tapes are useful for children weighing thirty five kilograms or less.

26
00:03:36.428 --> 00:03:38.158
And one more trap.

27
00:03:38.158 --> 00:03:55.353
The RCP 105 Initiation of Mechanical Ventilation deck also has a formula of age divided by four, plus four, but that one estimates the normal alveolar to arterial oxygen gradient.

28
00:03:55.353 --> 00:03:57.467
It is not a tube size.

29
00:03:58.867 --> 00:04:00.945
Pause and work this out.

30
00:04:00.945 --> 00:04:04.064
You need a tube for a four year old.

31
00:04:04.064 --> 00:04:09.867
What uncuffed size, what cuffed size, and what depth from the lips?

32
00:04:10.767 --> 00:04:16.767
[6-second practice pause]

33
00:04:17.267 --> 00:04:19.743
Four divided by four is one.

34
00:04:19.743 --> 00:04:32.212
Plus four gives five point zero for an uncuffed tube, and plus three point five gives four point five for a cuffed tube, half a size smaller.

35
00:04:32.212 --> 00:04:38.314
The depth from the lips is the age plus ten, so fourteen centimeters.

36
00:04:38.314 --> 00:04:42.117
Those two sizes are the deck's own example.

37
00:04:43.533 --> 00:04:50.295
No deck gives a neonatal size table, so this one is from Hess chapter twenty two.

38
00:04:50.295 --> 00:04:59.311
Estimate the size and depth from the infant's weight, or use the gestational age if the weight is not known.

39
00:04:59.311 --> 00:05:05.155
Under one thousand grams, or under thirty weeks, use a two point five.

40
00:05:05.155 --> 00:05:12.752
One thousand to two thousand grams, or thirty to thirty five weeks, use a three point zero.

41
00:05:12.752 --> 00:05:20.098
Two thousand to three thousand grams, or over thirty five weeks, use a three point five.

42
00:05:20.098 --> 00:05:24.773
Over three thousand grams, use three point five to four.

43
00:05:24.773 --> 00:05:34.707
The depth from the lips is the weight in kilograms plus six centimeters, so a one kilogram infant is seven centimeters.

44
00:05:34.707 --> 00:05:39.883
Then confirm by listening on both sides and with a chest film.

45
00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:50.126
RCP 203 Lesson 6a says to use uncuffed tubes for premature infants or infants under three kilograms.

46
00:05:50.126 --> 00:06:00.894
Otherwise, both cuffed and uncuffed tubes are acceptable in infants and children, and cuffed tubes are now routinely used.

47
00:06:00.894 --> 00:06:12.104
Hess says neonatal tubes are uncuffed, because the cricoid acts as a functional cuff, and pediatric tubes are sometimes cuffed.

48
00:06:12.104 --> 00:06:23.578
For a child's cuff pressure, the deck says usually no more than twenty to thirty centimeters of water, and Hess says under twenty.

49
00:06:23.578 --> 00:06:24.725
Use the deck.

50
00:06:26.133 --> 00:06:26.906
One more.

51
00:06:26.906 --> 00:06:31.113
A newborn weighs two thousand five hundred grams.

52
00:06:31.113 --> 00:06:39.783
Using the Hess table, what tube size do you choose, and using the depth rule, how deep from the lips?

53
00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:46.700
[6-second practice pause]

54
00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:57.649
Two thousand five hundred grams falls in the two thousand to three thousand gram row, so choose a three point five tube.

55
00:06:57.649 --> 00:07:08.534
The depth rule is the weight in kilograms plus six, so two point five plus six is eight point five centimeters from the lips.

56
00:07:08.534 --> 00:07:14.019
Then confirm with breath sounds on both sides and a chest film.

57
00:07:14.019 --> 00:07:20.550
This infant is under three kilograms, so RCP 203 says use an uncuffed tube.

58
00:07:21.967 --> 00:07:24.311
Here is the chapter in brief.

59
00:07:24.311 --> 00:07:43.875
Adults are sized by sex: seven to eight for women and eight to nine for men, with an initial depth of nineteen to twenty one or twenty one to twenty three centimeters at the teeth, confirmed on a film four to six centimeters above the carina.

60
00:07:43.875 --> 00:07:46.947
Use size eight or larger when you can.

61
00:07:46.947 --> 00:08:01.256
Children use the age divided by four plus four, uncuffed, or plus three point five, cuffed, with a depth of the age plus ten, or a length-based tape up to thirty five kilograms.

62
00:08:01.256 --> 00:08:08.128
Neonates are sized by weight or gestational age, with a depth of the weight plus six.

63
00:08:08.128 --> 00:08:12.817
Check the slides for the tables this guide could not read.
